\title{Proving a set uncountable}
\date{2026-07-16T15:07:58Z}
\tag{discrete}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{Are there any non-countable sets? How would we prove it if there were? Let’s use our superpower:}
##{
\begin{align*}
  &\phantom{\equiv} \neg \mathrm{Countable}(A) \\
  &\equiv \neg \exists f:\N \to A \st \mathrm{Bijective}(f) \\
  &\equiv \neg \exists f:\N \to A \st \mathrm{Injective}(f) \land
    \mathrm{Surjective}(f) \\
  &\equiv \forall f:\N \to A \st \neg \mathrm{Injective}(f) \lor
    \neg \mathrm{Surjective}(f)
\end{align*}
}

\p{So to prove that a set #{A} is not countable, we must show that for \em{every} possible function #{f} from #{\N} to #{A}, either #{f} is not injective or it is not surjective. Note that a function from #{\N} to #{A} is the same as an infinite list of values from the set #{A}. So we have to show that every possible infinite list of #{A} either has repeated elements, or leaves some elements out.}

